{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "201",
    "case_summary": "A 47-year-old Caucasian woman with a 23-year history of presumed multiple sclerosis—characterized by four episodes of optic neuritis since 1989, a longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis in 1995, persistently negative cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) oligoclonal bands, and unremarkable brain MRI scans—was treated with monthly intravenous methylprednisolone pulses and interferon-β. In January 2012, she presented with subacute onset of expressive aphasia and right-sided hemiparesis. On examination, she was alert with expressive language impairment and 3/5 strength in the right arm and leg; vital signs were normal. Laboratory studies demonstrated a normal complete blood count and metabolic panel. Brain MRI showed a large T2-hyperintense, tumefactive lesion in the left prefrontal white matter and smaller T2 lesions on the right without clear evidence of a disturbed blood–brain barrier. MR spectroscopy revealed an unchanged choline peak. CSF analysis was unremarkable, with no evidence of intrathecal immunoglobulin synthesis, no oligoclonal bands, and a normal cell count. Time-of-flight MR angiography showed no signs of vasculitis, and laboratory work-up revealed no clinical or serological evidence for connective tissue diseases. Serum aquaporin-4 antibody titre was 1:1000 by cell-based assay.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. NeuromyelitisOptica\nB. Malignant brain tumor was considered but excluded\nC. Central nervous system vasculitis was considered but excluded\nD. Connective tissue disease was considered but excluded\nE. Multiple sclerosis tumour\nF. Neuromyelitis optica was confirmed\nG. None\nH. None\n",
